module nfkbTranslocationDetection

manifest BoneMarrowCells
manifest mCSF_DifferentiationMedia
manifest PBS
manifest FibronectinSolution
manifest CompleteRPMI
manifest Hoechst33342
manifest TNFalpha
manifest LipidA
manifest Pam2CSK4
manifest R848_Resiquimod
manifest CpG_ODN1668

instructions:

// ============================================================
// Accurate Processing of Ultra-Short Immune Signals by Single
// Macrophages
// Mercado-Vasquez, Vizzard, Celiker, Xia, Wang, Padhi, Tay
// bioRxiv 2025.10.26.684377
// DOI: https://doi.org/10.1101/2025.10.26.684377
//
// Protocol: Primary bone-marrow-derived macrophage (BMDM)
// isolation and differentiation from mVenus-RelA (RelA^V/V)
// C57BL/6J transgenic mice, seeding into multilayer PDMS
// microfluidic culture chambers, nuclear staining, and
// short-pulse stimulation (1-second) with five NF-kB ligands
// (TNF, Lipid A, Pam2CSK4, R848, CpG) delivered by pneumatic
// valve control at 4 psi. NF-kB nuclear translocation of the
// mVenus-RelA p65 reporter is tracked by time-lapse
// epifluorescence microscopy (6-min intervals, 6 h total) and
// quantified as nuclear/cytoplasmic ratio.
//
// LIGANDS AND DOSES (from paper Methods/Figure 1A):
//   TNF alpha     10  ng/mL   -- cytokine (TNFR pathway)
//   Lipid A       10  ng/mL   -- bacterial TLR4 ligand
//   Pam2CSK4       3  ng/mL   -- bacterial TLR2/6 ligand
//   R848 Resiquimod 300 ng/mL -- viral TLR7 ligand (endosomal)
//   CpG ODN 1668  300 nM      -- DNA TLR9 ligand
//
// SCOPE NOTE: Each ligand stimulation arm is encoded as a
// sequential independent sub-experiment (STEP 4a-e) within
// a single main BB. In practice the five conditions are run
// in parallel across separate microfluidic chambers on the
// same chip; BioScript sequential encoding abstracts this.
// Each 1-second pulse is modelled as a 1s `mix` of the
// pre-loaded cell droplet with the ligand stimulus, which
// represents pneumatic valve opening (4 psi, PEEK tubing)
// for exactly 1 second to deliver the chemical pulse.
// The paper validates 1-second pulse delivery with 6%
// timing variability (CV = 0.06) across all analyzed cells.
//
// DURATION NOTES:
//   7-9 day BMDM differentiation -> 9 days = 12960m
//   4-6 h microfluidic attachment -> 6 h = 360m
//   Imaging window = 6 h = 360m (6-min frames over 60 cycles)
//   Overnight = 720m (not used here; explicit durations given)
//   BioScript duration tokens: s and m only (no h token)
//
// CFG STRUCTURE:
//   BB1  main             -- BMDM prep, device priming, cell
//                           seeding, nuclear staining, five
//                           1-second ligand pulse deliveries
//   BB2  responsePass     -- nfkbSignal > 0: NF-kB nuclear
//                           translocation detected; dispose
//                           stimulated cell droplets
//   BB3  responseFail     -- nfkbSignal <= 0: no detectable
//                           NF-kB response; drain droplets
//   BB4  finalCleanup     -- terminal: dispose QC signal,
//                           experiment ends
// ============================================================

// ============================================================
// BB1: main
// ============================================================

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 1: BMDM isolation and differentiation.
//   Bone marrow cells flushed from tibias and femurs of 6-12
//   week-old male and female RelA^V/V C57BL/6J mice and
//   resuspended in complete RPMI supplemented with 20 ng/mL
//   m-CSF. Cells differentiated for 7-9 days (encoded as
//   9 days = 12960 min) at 37 C / 5% CO2 before harvesting.
//   m-CSF: Peprotech murine m-CSF, reconstituted in ultrapure
//   water, stored at -80 C.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

rawBoneMarrow          = dispense 1 units of BoneMarrowCells
mCSF_Media             = dispense 1 units of mCSF_DifferentiationMedia
differentiatingBMDMs   = mix rawBoneMarrow with mCSF_Media for 5s

// 9-day differentiation at 37 C / 5% CO2 (= 12960 min)
heat differentiatingBMDMs at 37c for 12960m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 2: Microfluidic device priming with fibronectin.
//   Multilayer PDMS device fabricated from silicon wafer
//   molds (10:1 PDMS monomer:catalyst; oxygen plasma bonding,
//   overnight bake). Chambers pre-incubated with 0.2 mg/mL
//   fibronectin in PBS to promote cell adhesion.
//   `mix` abstracts the channel-flow fibronectin coating step.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

fibronectin            = dispense 1 units of FibronectinSolution
pbsPrime               = dispense 1 units of PBS
coatedChamber          = mix fibronectin with pbsPrime for 5m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 3: BMDM seeding and equilibration.
//   Harvested BMDMs on day 7-9, resuspended in complete RPMI
//   (phenol red-free), and seeded at 10^6 cells/mL into
//   fibronectin-coated microfluidic chambers. Cells left
//   unperturbed for 4-6 h (encoded as 6 h = 360 min) at
//   37 C / 5% CO2 until attached and equilibrated.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

harvestedBMDMs         = mix differentiatingBMDMs with coatedChamber for 1m

// 6-hour attachment and equilibration at 37 C (= 360 min)
heat harvestedBMDMs at 37c for 360m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 4: Nuclear tracking stain.
//   Cells stained with 1 uM Hoechst 33342 (Invitrogen) in
//   phenol red-free RPMI per producer instructions to enable
//   nuclear segmentation and cell tracking throughout the
//   time-lapse imaging run.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

hoechstStain           = dispense 1 units of Hoechst33342
completeRPMI_Stain     = dispense 1 units of CompleteRPMI
stainedBMDMs           = mix harvestedBMDMs with hoechstStain for 1m
equilibratedBMDMs      = mix stainedBMDMs with completeRPMI_Stain for 5m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 5a: TNF alpha 1-second pulse stimulation.
//   Proinflammatory cytokine TNF alpha (R&D Systems murine
//   TNFalpha), 10 ng/mL in complete phenol red-free RPMI.
//   Stored on ice during experiment; delivered at 4 psi via
//   PEEK tubing (VICI). Pneumatic valve open for 1 second.
//   Models intracellular (cell-cell) cytokine signaling
//   via TNFR1 (CD120a) pathway.
//   `mix for 1s` abstracts 1-second pneumatic valve pulse.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

tnfStimulus            = dispense 1 units of TNFalpha
tnfStimulatedCells     = mix equilibratedBMDMs with tnfStimulus for 1s

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 5b: Lipid A 1-second pulse stimulation.
//   Ultrapure Lipid A from E. coli O111:B4 (Invivogen),
//   10 ng/mL. Bacterial TLR4 ligand. Analogous to LPS
//   component targeting the extracellular receptor TLR4.
//   Delivered at 4 psi for 1 second.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

lipidAStimulus         = dispense 1 units of LipidA
lipidAStimulatedCells  = mix equilibratedBMDMs with lipidAStimulus for 1s

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 5c: Pam2CSK4 1-second pulse stimulation.
//   PAM2CSK4 (Invivogen), 3 ng/mL. Diacylated lipopeptide
//   analog of bacterial outer membrane components. Induces
//   TLR2/TLR6 stable heterodimer formation (m-shape complex)
//   activating MyD88/TIRAP downstream NF-kB via IKK module.
//   Delivered at 4 psi for 1 second.
//   NOTE: Pam2CSK4 shows highest NF-kB response amplitude
//   and maximal channel capacity (CC_max ~0.82) under 1-second
//   pulse, surpassing Lipid A which is most potent under
//   persistent stimulation (key finding of the paper).
// ------------------------------------------------------------

pam2csk4Stimulus       = dispense 1 units of Pam2CSK4
pamStimulatedCells     = mix equilibratedBMDMs with pam2csk4Stimulus for 1s

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 5d: R848 (Resiquimod) 1-second pulse stimulation.
//   R848 Resiquimod (Invivogen), 300 ng/mL. Small molecule
//   viral RNA mimic that binds TLR7 located in the endosome
//   (intracellular receptor pathway). Induces TLR7 homodimer
//   formation activating MyD88 downstream. Modelled here as
//   endosomal receptor-mediated pathogen detection.
//   Delivered at 4 psi for 1 second.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

r848Stimulus           = dispense 1 units of R848_Resiquimod
r848StimulatedCells    = mix equilibratedBMDMs with r848Stimulus for 1s

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 5e: CpG ODN 1668 1-second pulse stimulation.
//   ODN 1668 CpG (Invivogen), 300 nM. Single-stranded CpG
//   DNA dinucleotide targeting TLR9. TLR9 is an endosomal
//   receptor for foreign/pathogenic DNA. Lowest pairwise
//   mutual information (MI = 0) vs CpG, indicating weakest
//   distinguishability from TNF under 1-second pulse.
//   Delivered at 4 psi for 1 second.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

cpgStimulus            = dispense 1 units of CpG_ODN1668
cpgStimulatedCells     = mix equilibratedBMDMs with cpgStimulus for 1s

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 6: Time-lapse NF-kB live-cell imaging.
//   Nikon Ti2 epifluorescence microscope; 20x magnification;
//   Hamamatsu ORCA-Flash4.0 V2 camera. mVenus-RelA channel
//   (508 nm excitation) and Hoechst channel (395 nm) acquired
//   every 6 minutes for 6 hours = 60 imaging cycles = 360 min
//   total. No photobleaching or phototoxicity observed.
//   Custom MATLAB image processing software segments nuclei
//   via Hoechst, extracts nuclear/cytoplasmic mVenus-RelA
//   ratio per cell per frame. > 200 individual cells analyzed
//   per stimulation condition.
//
//   QC gate: nfkbTranslocationDetection module abstracts the
//   MATLAB image analysis pipeline and reports a positive
//   signal when NF-kB nuclear translocation peak amplitude
//   exceeds the activation threshold (2 s.d. above unstimulated
//   mean nuclear/cytoplasmic ratio). The Pam2CSK4 condition
//   is used as the QC reference (strongest responder under
//   1-second pulse). All five stimulated cell populations
//   (STEP 5a-e) are pooled into a single merged imaging
//   droplet for the QC detect call.
//
//   NOTE ON COMPILER CONSTRAINT: The five stimulated cell
//   populations (tnfStimulatedCells, lipidAStimulatedCells,
//   pamStimulatedCells, r848StimulatedCells, cpgStimulatedCells)
//   are merged sequentially via distinct intermediate variables
//   (no variable reassignment) before the detect call.
//   This abstracts the single Nikon Ti2 field-of-view that
//   images all five stimulation chambers during the 6-hour
//   time-lapse run. Detection duration = 360 min.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

// Sequential merging of all five stimulated cell populations
// for the unified imaging and QC detect call.
// Distinct intermediate variable names required (no reassignment).

imagingPool_ab     = mix tnfStimulatedCells with lipidAStimulatedCells for 1m
imagingPool_abc    = mix imagingPool_ab with pamStimulatedCells for 1m
imagingPool_abcd   = mix imagingPool_abc with r848StimulatedCells for 1m
imagingPool_all    = mix imagingPool_abcd with cpgStimulatedCells for 1m

// 6-hour live imaging incubation at 37 C / 5% CO2 (= 360 min)
// Microfluidic in-situ microbiome box maintains temperature,
// humidity and CO2 conditions throughout the imaging run.
heat imagingPool_all at 37c for 360m

// NF-kB nuclear translocation QC detect (6-hour imaging window)
// nfkbTranslocationDetection: custom MATLAB-based image analysis
// module quantifying mVenus-RelA nuclear/cytoplasmic ratio.
// Positive signal: activation threshold exceeded (peak > 2 s.d.
// above unstimulated mean) in >= 1 detected cell.
nfkbSignal = detect nfkbTranslocationDetection on imagingPool_all for 360m

// ============================================================
// BB2 / BB3: NF-kB translocation QC gate
// Pass (BB2): nfkbSignal > 0 -> nuclear NF-kB translocation
//             detected above activation threshold; ligand
//             delivery and cell response confirmed; dispose
//             stimulated cell imaging pool.
// Fail (BB3): nfkbSignal <= 0 -> no detectable NF-kB response
//             (cells non-viable, ligand delivery failure, or
//             imaging artifact); drain imaging pool.
// Both branches converge at BB4 (finalCleanup).
// ============================================================

if (nfkbSignal > 0) {
    dispose imagingPool_all
} else {
    drain imagingPool_all
}

// ============================================================
// BB4: finalCleanup (terminal)
// Dispose NF-kB QC signal. Experiment ends.
// Downstream quantitative analysis (8 dynamic NF-kB features:
// early AUC, late AUC, amplitude, speed, duration, oscillations,
// activation time, time to peak; plus k-means subpopulation
// clustering into early responders, late responders, and
// non-responders; plus channel capacity and mutual information
// calculations) performed off-chip in MATLAB 2024a using
// custom software (github.com/tay-lab/Accurate-processing-
// of-ultra-short-immune-signals-by-single-macrophages).
// ============================================================

dispose nfkbSignal

// [terminal -- no further instructions; exit node for CFG]
